--TEST--
Core\Regex::split reads limit as Core\Str::split does and applies keepEmpty after it, so a zero limit, a negative one and a limit beside the flag each part from preg_split
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
`limit` is Core\Str::split's word and drops empty pieces after it, not preg_split's — nvs_core_regex_split's doc comment
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The three places § 5's `split` row parts from `preg_split`, all one
// decision: `limit` counts the pieces the split *produced*, and it means what
// the other member named `split` means by it. `nvs_core_regex_split`'s doc
// comment is the home of the reasoning; this case is the measurement, and the
// agreement case beside it — `regex-split-matches-preg_split-including-its-
// limit-and-its-empty-pieces` — holds every row where the two do agree.
//
// PHP's answer is written beside each row because a divergence case with a
// frozen expectation cannot show it any other way, and a divergence nobody can
// see the other half of is indistinguishable from a bug.
final class Render {
    public static function of(array<string> $pieces): string {
        return Core\Str::join($pieces, "|") . " (" . (Core\Arr::count($pieces) as string) . ")";
    }
}

// A limit of zero is one piece — the subject unsplit — because that is what
// `Core\Str::split(…, {limit: 0})` answers, and two members named `split`
// disagreeing about one word is the cost this trades away. `preg_split` reads
// `0` as "no limit" and answers `a|b|c|d (4)`.
echo Render::of(Core\Regex::split("a1b2c3d", "\\d", {limit: 0})), "\n";

// A negative limit drops that many pieces off the end. `preg_split` documents
// only `-1` as "no limit" and gives no other negative a meaning: PHP 8.5
// answers `a1b2c3d (1)` to both rows below, which is the subject unsplit, and
// there is no rule there to be compatible with.
echo Render::of(Core\Regex::split("a1b2c3d", "\\d", {limit: -2})), "\n";
echo Render::of(Core\Regex::split("a1b2c3d", "\\d", {limit: -9})), "\n";

// `keepEmpty: false` runs *after* the limit, so the limit is a bound on the
// split and not on the answer — a reading under which the two options compose
// rather than interact. `PREG_SPLIT_NO_EMPTY` beside a limit makes the limit
// count surviving pieces instead, so PHP keeps splitting past it: it answers
// `a|,b, (2)` to the first row and `a|b (2)` to the second, third and fourth.
// The fourth is where the two happen to coincide, and the first three are why
// that coincidence is not the rule.
echo Render::of(Core\Regex::split(",a,,b,", ",", {limit: 2, keepEmpty: false})), "\n";
echo Render::of(Core\Regex::split(",a,,b,", ",", {limit: 3, keepEmpty: false})), "\n";
echo Render::of(Core\Regex::split(",a,,b,", ",", {limit: 5, keepEmpty: false})), "\n";
echo Render::of(Core\Regex::split(",a,,b,", ",", {limit: -1, keepEmpty: false})), "\n";
--EXPECT--
a1b2c3d (1)
a|b (2)
 (0)
a,,b, (1)
a|,b, (2)
a|b (2)
a|b (2)
